    "result_text": "I implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py`. The existing tests pass (4 passed) and a manual check gave the expected output, but I added no tests for the new function.\n\n**How it works:**\n- It looks up documents with `retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})`. This is the standard way to call any LangChain retriever, so it works for both `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. Passing the tag in `config` puts it on the lookup's trace run.\n- It goes through the documents in the order the retriever returned them and skips any whose `metadata[\"source\"]` has already been used. It stops once it has `MAX_SOURCES` entries.\n- Each entry is `\"[n] <source>\\n<text with surrounding whitespace removed>\"`. Entries are joined with `\"\\n\\n\"`, so there's one blank line between them. If nothing is found, it returns `\"\"`.\n\n**Manual check:**\n- A duplicate `vpn.md` document was dropped.\n- The three entries were numbered `[1]`\u2013`[3]` in the retriever's order.\n- A query with no matches (`\"kubernetes\"`) returned `\"\"`.\n- The tag reached the trace callbacks. Callbacks set on the retriever object itself are ignored by `invoke` in langchain-core 1.6.5, so traces need to attach their handlers through `config`, which is how LangSmith tracing works anyway.\n\nNo dependencies were added. One cleanup is left: I couldn't delete my scratch file `scratch_check.py` because the `rm` needed your approval. Please delete it."
  },
